# [the treadmill]($section.id('problem').attrs('page'))

Your agent writes a bug. You spot it. You say _"please don't do that"_. It fixes it.

```=html
<div class="speech left">I swallowed an exception with a bare <code>except: pass</code></div>
<div class="speech right">fixed! ✨</div>
```

Next week, a different session, a different file:

```=html
<div class="speech left">I swallowed an exception with a bare <code>except: pass</code></div>
```

>[]($block.attrs('big'))
>You fixed the [instance]($text.attrs('hl')).\
>The [category]($text.attrs('hl')) is still alive.

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>Agents don't remember last Tuesday. Your codebase does.

# [the big idea]($section.id('idea').attrs('page'))

>[]($block.attrs('big'))
>When you find a mistake, don't fix _it_.\
>Fix [every future version of it]($text.attrs('hl')).

>[]($block.attrs('box'))
>Ask one question:
>
>_"What rule would have caught this **automatically**?"_
>
>Then write that rule. Lint it. Run it everywhere.

It's **kaizen** for codebases that are written by robots. Yes, Toyota did this in the 1950s. We have more YAML. Toyota called it **poka yoke**: mistake proofing. Make the wrong thing impossible, or at least _loud_.

# [the loop]($section.id('loop').attrs('page'))

>[]($block.attrs('loop'))
>1. **spot** a mistake in a diff, a review, an incident
>2. **name** the category, not the line
>3. **encode** it as a deterministic check
>4. **run** it in the LSP, the watcher, the CI
>5. **let the agent self correct** from the error message

>[]($block.attrs('arrow'))
>↺ forever

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>Each lap is small. Five minutes. One rule. The point is that you never run the same lap twice.

# [why exponential?]($section.id('why').attrs('page'))

A fix touches **one** line.

A rule touches **every line** written by **every agent** in **every session**, forever.

```=html
<svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 260 150" role="img" aria-label="Fixing instances grows linearly, adding rules compounds">
  <path d="M20 135 H250 M20 135 V10" class="axis"/>
  <path d="M20 132 L245 95" class="linear"/>
  <path d="M20 132 C140 130 200 100 242 18" class="expo"/>
  <text x="150" y="130" class="label">fixing instances</text>
  <text x="120" y="45" class="label">adding rules</text>
  <text x="200" y="148" class="label small">time →</text>
</svg>
```

Rules compound like interest. Your agents get _better_ without getting _smarter_, because the space of wrong answers keeps shrinking.

# [it cuts both ways]($section.id('both-ways').attrs('page'))

Agents copy what they see. Every pattern in your repo is a template for the next thousand lines.

```=html
<svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 260 150" role="img" aria-label="With rules quality compounds upwards, without rules it compounds downwards">
  <path d="M20 75 H250 M20 140 V10" class="axis"/>
  <path d="M20 75 C140 74 200 55 242 12" class="better"/>
  <path d="M20 75 C140 76 200 95 242 138" class="expo"/>
  <text x="120" y="38" class="label">ratchet up</text>
  <text x="110" y="118" class="label">rot down</text>
  <text x="200" y="88" class="label small">time →</text>
</svg>
```

One sloppy `except: pass` becomes ten. Ten becomes the house style. The agent isn't wrong, it's _consistent_.

>[]($block.attrs('big'))
>No rules isn't standing still.\
>It's [compounding the other way]($text.attrs('hl')).

# [harden the codebase, not the agent]($section.id('codebase').attrs('page'))

Agents come and go. Claude Code today, Codex tomorrow, something new next quarter.

```=html
<div class="stations">
  <div><b>tied to one agent</b><span>prompts, skills, memory, clever system messages</span></div>
  <div class="hot"><b>lives in the repo</b><span>lint config, types, tests, CI, watchers</span></div>
</div>
```

The repo is the **shared context**. Every agent and every human reads it, every session, no setup required.

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>Tune the agent and you improve one tool. Harden the codebase and you improve all of them, including the ones that don't exist yet.

>[]($block.attrs('credit'))
>More: [hardening codebases for agentic coding](https://benomahony.com/blog/hardening-codebases-for-agentic-coding/)

# [the enforcement ladder]($section.id('ladder').attrs('page'))

Not all "rules" are equal. Climb as high as you can.

>[]($block.attrs('ladder'))
>- **unrepresentable** types make it impossible
>- **type checker** fails the build
>- **lint rule** fails with a fix hint
>- **test** catches it after the fact
>- **review comment** catches it sometimes
>- **AGENTS.md** prose: a polite wish

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>Prose in a prompt is a suggestion. A failing check is a fact.

>[]($block.attrs('credit'))
>More: [don't confuse guidance for guardrails](https://benomahony.com/blog/my-10-rules-for-agentic-engineering/#dont-confuse-guidance-for-guardrails)

# [actionable errors are just in time prompts]($section.id('jit').attrs('page'))

```=html
<div class="stations">
  <div><b>AGENTS.md</b><span>loaded every session, all of it, relevant or not. Burns context. Easy to skim past.</span></div>
  <div class="hot"><b>an actionable error</b><span>appears only when the mistake happens, on the exact line, with the exact fix.</span></div>
</div>
```

>[]($block.attrs('big'))
>Don't front load the context.\
>Deliver it [the moment it's needed]($text.attrs('hl')).

A rule that never fires costs zero tokens. A rule that fires is the most relevant prompt the agent will see all day.

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>The best prompt arrives exactly when the agent is about to be wrong.

# [anatomy of a good rule]($section.id('anatomy').attrs('page'))

The error message **is the prompt**. The agent reads it and fixes itself.

```=html
<pre class="bad"><code>error: banned import</code></pre>
<p class="verdict">😐 the agent guesses</p>
<pre class="good"><code>error: `pandas` is banned.
  Use duckdb for SQL style queries
  or polars for dataframes.
  See docs/data.md</code></pre>
<p class="verdict">😎 the agent knows</p>
```

>[]($block.attrs('checks'))
>- says **what** is wrong
>- says **what to do instead**
>- points **where** to read more
>- is **deterministic**: same input, same answer

# [real rules, real cheap]($section.id('examples').attrs('page'))

Ban a library with **ruff**:

```
[tool.ruff.lint.flake8-tidy-imports.banned-api]
"pandas".msg = "Use duckdb or polars instead."
"requests".msg = "Use httpx with a timeout."
```

Ban a pattern with **ast-grep**:

```
id: no-print-in-lib
language: python
rule:
  pattern: print($$$ARGS)
message: Use the logger from app.log, not print().
severity: error
```

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>Most mistakes are already a rule someone wrote. Turn it on before you write your own.

# [quality ratchets]($section.id('ratchets').attrs('page'))

A ratchet only turns one way. Pick a number: lint violations, type errors, coverage. Commit it. CI fails if it gets worse and tightens it when it gets better.

```
lint violations
mon  412
tue  398  ✓ ratchet tightened
wed  401  ✗ CI fails: 401 > 398
thu  371  ✓ ratchet tightened
```

No big bang cleanup. Turn the new rule on _today_, freeze the existing violations, block every new one, and let the count only fall.

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>Every gain gets locked in. Nobody, human or agent, can quietly give it back.

>[]($block.attrs('credit'))
>Quality Ratchets are from [_Building AI Agent Platforms_](https://buildingaiagentplatforms.com) by Ben O'Mahony and Fabian Nonnenmacher (O'Reilly). See also [every failure becomes a ratchet](https://benomahony.com/blog/my-10-rules-for-agentic-engineering/#every-failure-becomes-a-ratchet).

# [where rules live]($section.id('where').attrs('page'))

Run them where the agent will trip over them [fastest]($text.attrs('hl')).

```=html
<div class="stations">
  <div class="hot"><b>LSP</b><span>diagnostics on every save, for every agent and every editor</span></div>
  <div class="hot"><b>watch mode daemons</b><span>tests and linters rerun on every change, failures handed straight back</span></div>
  <div><b>pre commit</b><span>nothing broken gets saved</span></div>
  <div><b>CI</b><span>the backstop that never sleeps</span></div>
</div>
```

Agent hooks tie you to one agent. LSPs and daemons live with the codebase and work for all of them, humans included.

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>The shorter the loop, the less the agent wanders off building on a broken idea.

# [overwatch]($section.id('overwatch').attrs('page'))

The fastest loop is the one nobody has to start. Run your tests and linters in **watch mode**, right beside the agent.

>[]($block.attrs('checks'))
>- full suite runs first, so you start green
>- every save reruns what changed
>- failures go **straight back to the agent**, before it moves on
>- the turn can't end while the build is red

```
overwatch zig ✓ 4/4 steps succeeded; 48/48 tests passed
```

```
/plugin install overwatch --marketplace benomahony/overwatch
```

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>Works with Claude Code, Codex and Neovim. [github.com/benomahony/overwatch](https://github.com/benomahony/overwatch)

# [thin slices, straight to trunk]($section.id('slices').attrs('page'))

Small enough to review in a minute. Small enough to revert in a second. On main many times a day.

```=html
<div class="stations">
  <div><b>harness engineering</b><span>worktree swarms, orchestrator agents, forty parallel branches, merge queues for robots</span></div>
  <div class="hot"><b>thin slices</b><span>one small change, green, on trunk, next</span></div>
</div>
```

>[]($block.attrs('big'))
>Most harness engineering is a workaround for [slices that are too thick]($text.attrs('hl')).

Trunk is the shared context. Every slice that lands keeps it current for every agent and every human.

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>We once had a feature branch live for 45 minutes. [We now call that long lived.](https://benomahony.com/blog/the-45-min-long-lived-feature-branch/)

# [tapas teams]($section.id('tapas').attrs('page'))

Two pizza teams were sized for humans typing. With agents, the plates got smaller.

```=html
<div class="stations">
  <div><b>two pizza team</b><span>eight people, a backlog, a sprint, a demo in two weeks</span></div>
  <div class="hot"><b>tapas team</b><span>three or four people across product, design and engineering, mobbing with agents, lots of small plates, everyone tastes everything</span></div>
</div>
```

The only number that matters:

>[]($block.attrs('big'))
>time to [first customer feedback]($text.attrs('hl'))

Not lines of code. Not PRs merged. How fast does a real person tell you whether it's any good?

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>Agents made building cheap. Being wrong is still expensive. Find out sooner.

# [careful now]($section.id('careful').attrs('page'))

>[]($block.attrs('warn'))
>- **noisy rules are bugs.** A false positive teaches agents (and humans) to add `noqa` everywhere.
>- **prune.** Hundreds of prose rules make agents follow _fewer_ of them. Lint rules only speak when broken, so they scale. Prose doesn't.
>- **escape hatches need reasons.** `# noqa: BLE001 retry loop logs it`
>- **don't make an LLM your linter.** Pass or fail, every time, for free.

# [try it monday]($section.id('monday').attrs('page'))

>[]($block.attrs('todo'))
>- keep a **"that again?!"** list for one week
>- for each entry, find an existing lint rule
>- no rule exists? write one with ast-grep or semgrep
>- run tests and lint in watch mode with overwatch
>- make CI fail on it, and ratchet the count down
>- delete the matching line from AGENTS.md 🎉

# [wait, isn't this just continuous improvement?]($section.id('rebrand').attrs('page'))

...yes. 🤫

>[]($block.attrs('rebrand'))
>| Toyota, 1950s | us, now |
>|---|---|
>| kaizen | exponential engineering |
>| poka yoke | a lint rule |
>| andon cord | overwatch going red |
>| jidoka: stop the line | the turn can't end while red |
>| five whys | five whys, but the agent answers instantly and confidently |
>| the new hire | the new hire, typing 400 words a second, with no memory of yesterday |

The ideas aren't new. The **speed** is. When your juniors write a thousand lines an hour, a missing rule doesn't cost you a bug. It costs you a _pattern_.

>[]($block.attrs('aside'))
>Please don't tell W. Edwards Deming we put a GPU in it.
